  "id": "herati_dutar_chaharbeiti",
  "name": "Herati music (Herat dutar and chaharbeiti song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Regional music of Herat in western Afghanistan, documented by John Baily in his study of the city's professional musicians and by Veronica Doubleday among Herati women. Men's local music centres on the long-necked dutar, which Herati players enlarged in the twentieth century from two strings to three and later fourteen, with a goblet or frame drum; women sing chaharbeiti quatrains and wedding songs to the daireh frame drum, for which the doira card stands; the dutar card, set to its Turkmen two-string folk form (the nearest setting it offers), stands for the Herati dutar.",
  "recipe": "classic modern herati music, ballad Poetry, med east poetic branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. turkmen long necked bagshy tradition turkmen bagshy song accompaniment dutar, doira frame drum, zerbaghali. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
